license: MIT
keywords:
  - strange loop
  - self-model
  - language models
  - interpretability
  - global workspace
  - J-space
  - self-prediction
  - representation geometry
abstract: >-
  Four loops that feed a compact description of a language model's own internal state back into the model (Qwen3
  1.7B-32B). Loops through a self-description keep what they can regenerate rather than what is their own; a learning
  self-model's surprise is a learned exit from attractors; self-specificity needs private state. A trained
  self-describing loop around the model's own thinking, under discriminative pressure to be predictable to its own
  self-model, represents its own time as a multi-frequency rotation, or a persistent
  identity when it must tell itself from others; a fed-back self-forecast is enacted, making the self-model a cause.
